# ===========================================================================
# LINE Bot credentials — TEMPLATE
# ===========================================================================
# This file is a TEMPLATE and is safe to commit: it contains no real secrets.
#
# To use it:
#   1. Copy this file and name the copy ".env"   (no ".example" on the end)
#   2. Replace the placeholder values below with your real ones
#   3. Never commit .env — .gitignore already blocks it
#
#      Windows PowerShell:  Copy-Item .env.example .env
#      Git Bash:            cp .env.example .env
#
# Where these come from: https://developers.line.biz/console/
#   Create a Provider, then a "Messaging API" channel, then open the channel.
# ===========================================================================


# --- Channel access token --------------------------------------------------
# Console location: "Messaging API" tab -> "Channel access token (long-lived)"
#                   -> press Issue
# Proves to LINE that your server is allowed to send messages as this bot.
# Very long (~170 characters) and usually ends with "=".
#
# Treat it like a password. If it leaks, press Issue again in the console —
# that invalidates the old one immediately.
LINE_CHANNEL_ACCESS_TOKEN=your_channel_access_token_here


# --- Channel secret --------------------------------------------------------
# Console location: "Basic settings" tab -> "Channel secret"
# NOTE: a DIFFERENT tab from the token above. This is the most common mix-up.
#
# Used to verify that an incoming webhook really came from LINE and not from
# someone who guessed your URL. Short — a 32-character hex string.
LINE_CHANNEL_SECRET=your_channel_secret_here


# --- Optional tuning -------------------------------------------------------
# How long to wait (seconds) after the last message before replying. Lets a
# photo and a caption sent moments apart be treated as one request.
# The project doc recommends 2.5-3. Leave it unset to use the 2.5 default.
# DEBOUNCE_SECONDS=2.5
